WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.870
Most of us have held at least one soul-sucking
job in our lives.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.820
Luckily, there's always the option of quitting.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.100
Movie stars rarely have that option.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.950
Bailing for them is more complicated than
simply leaving.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.540
There are potential lawsuits and almost always
big financial penalties.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.660
All of which is why actors usually end up
toughing it out.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.970
Still, some actors get pushed to the breaking
point and come just short of actually quitting

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:24.900
even for some movies that are regarded as
classics today.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.620
Here are actors who threatened to quit their
most famous role.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.820
Wayne's World was filmed on a relatively modest
budget, but is still the only movie inspired

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.420
by a Saturday Night Live skit that's grossed
over $100,000,000.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:41.000
Mike Myers had come up with the concept as
a pre-teen, debuted it on a Canadian variety

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.620
show, and perfected it on SNL, so the character
was clearly dear to Myers' heart.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.800
One famous scene, where Wayne and his friends
are belting out Queen's "Bohemian Rhapsody"

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.720
in an AMC Pacer, was even written as an homage
to his teenage years.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.680
"I think we'll go with a little Bohemian Rhapsody,
gentlemen.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:58.480
... Good call."

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.580
He felt the song was "inherently comedic"
and loved the thought of living out his memories

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.449
of headbanging during the instrumental break
and singing along.

00:01:05.449 --> 00:01:07.820
But director Penelope Spheeris had different
ideas.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:11.800
The operatic tune seemed like an odd pick
to her, so she suggested a different song

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:12.300
instead.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.580
Myers put his foot down and threatened to
quit if Queen was cut.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:19.950
In the end, Myers won, the scene became iconic,
and "Bohemian Rhapsody" returned to the Billboard

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.660
charts nearly 20 years after its debut.

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.860
"Shwinnnng."

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.980
How the Grinch Stole Christmas might have
been painful to watch, but it was even more

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:31.800
painful for Jim Carrey to act in.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.939
The elaborate Grinch costume he wore was made
of itchy green yak hair, extensive facial

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:41.360
prosthetics, and yellow contacts that appeared
as big as Frisbees and reportedly felt like

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.320
"knives in the eye."

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.680
The first day on the set of The Grinch, Carrey's
makeup took a full eight and a half hours

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.280
to apply.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.400
After he went back his trailer, Carrey kicked
a hole in the wall, and told director Ron

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.600
Howard he couldn't do the movie.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:56.380
In an interview on The Graham Norton Show,
he had harsh words for the experience.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:02.280
"When I did the Grinch, I was literally- the
makeup was like being buried alive every day.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.080
How long did it take?

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.440
The first day was eight and a half hours."

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.240
Carrey was only persuaded to stay when the
production hired someone who trained CIA operatives

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:13.400
to withstand torture to assist Carrey in dealing
with the daily ordeal.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.580
The key lesson was in pain deferment, and
the actor often punched himself in the leg

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.720
if he was having a particularly hard time.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.100
Carrey remembers the hundred days spent in
the costume as a "real lesson in zen…it's

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.620
amazing what humans can endure."

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.060
In the end, it seems the torture was well
worth it: The Grinch earned more than $345

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.849
million in worldwide box office sales, making
it the second-highest-grossing Christmas movie

00:02:34.849 --> 00:02:35.580
of all time.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.640
"Ha ha ha."

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.750
Emma Watson landed the role of Hermione Granger
in Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone at

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.800
age nine, and quickly became a fan favorite
for her layered portrayal of the character.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:57.260
But when Watson's contract came up for renewal
as the fifth installment of the series was

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.980
being shot, rumors swirled that she was ready
to quit.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:00.920
The problem?

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:05.099
Starring in the Harry Potter films wasn't
going to leave Watson time for a college education.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:11.600
"I had a real fight on my hands to ensure
that I was able to go to university and take

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:12.180
my A-levels."

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.060
The original schedule wouldn't have left time
for Watson to do any of that, and she was

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.460
willing to quit over the matter.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.860
Luckily, the studio reworked the shoot to
accommodate Watson's educational aspirations.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.920
Fast forward to the happy ending: She continued
to play the beloved character in the final

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.420
films and earned a degree in English literature
from Brown University.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:30.940
"Look at you.

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:32.500
Playing with your little cards.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:33.520
Pathetic!

00:03:34.220 --> 00:03:35.940
We've got final exams coming up soon."

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.860
Brad Pitt is known for being an affable, charismatic
guy.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.640
But one look at him in Interview with the
Vampire and you can see he's not his normal

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.140
self.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.320
When an Entertainment Weekly reporter mentioned
that he looked miserable in the film, Pitt

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:49.360
replied, "I am miserable.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:52.880
Six months in the f------ dark… contact
lenses, the make-up…"

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.510
It seems Pitt signed on to play Louis because
he was intrigued by the character's journey

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.500
of self-discovery.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.880
Unfortunately, when he got the actual script
two weeks before filming started, everything

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.500
he loved about Louis had been cut.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:08.019
Now, his role was basically to sit back and
watch Lestat, played by Tom Cruise.

00:04:08.019 --> 00:04:10.370
One day during filming, Pitt decided he was
done.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.629
He called the producer, said he couldn't do
it anymore, and asked how much it would cost

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:14.360
him to get out.

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.660
The answer was a cool $40 million.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.320
Unsurprisingly, Pitt stayed on, and his character
is best remembered as giving Kirsten Dunst

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:22.220
her first kiss.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.320
Unfortunately, it was about as pleasant for
her as the filming was for Pitt.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.930
"You had a screen kiss with Brad Pitt when
you were eleven."

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.780
"Although, I thought it was disgusting.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:34.420
I was eleven!"

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:39.960
The Fast and Furious franchise has spawned
a slew of sequels, earned billions worldwide,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.050
and launched the careers of the original picture's
ensemble cast, including Michelle Rodriguez.

00:04:45.050 --> 00:04:50.220
But Rodriguez's portrayal of streetwise racer
Letty Ortiz was almost a non-starter: It seems

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.620
Rodriguez signed on despite having a major
beef with her character's story arc.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.410
Having only worked on a single film before,
she was used to working out character motivations

00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:58.980
on set.

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:02.700
But when Rodriguez mentioned she didn't think
it was realistic for Letty to be caught in

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:06.210
a cheesy love triangle, producers were having
none of it.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:10.250
Rodriguez cried, threatened to quit, and begged
the production not to sue her when she left.

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:14.620
Luckily, with the support of co-star Vin Diesel,
the script was eventually changed and Rodriguez's

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.300
Hollywood dream and portrayal of Letty Ortiz
lived to fight another day.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.240
Jessica Alba's performance as Sue Storm in
Fantastic Four: Rise of the Silver Surfer

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.080
might have earned her a Razzie nomination
for Worst Actress, but that may not have been

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.600
entirely her fault.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.880
According to Alba, director Tim Story complained
that her appearance in an emotional scene

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:45.830
looked too real and painful, telling her to
"cry pretty."

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:49.940
The experience was so traumatizing that that
Alba considered quitting all together.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:51.240
In a later interview, Alba said:

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.240
"I hated it.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.340
I really hated it.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:54.340
So I just said f--- it.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.410
I don't care about this business anymore."

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:00.410
Alba didn't quit the role, but afterwards
she pivoted from being an A-List actress to

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.320
founding the Honest Company, a line of beauty
products.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.780
The Shining is an undisputed horror classic.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.340
Unfortunately, while millions of fans have
delighted in rewatching the film, there's

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:14.630
at least one person who wasn't exactly happy
with the film: Shelley Duvall, who played

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:15.500
Wendy Torrance.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:19.620
Duvall once characterized working with the
famed director as "almost unbearable" and

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:23.050
"excruciating" in an interview with film critic
Roger Ebert.

00:06:23.050 --> 00:06:27.210
In Stanley Kubrick: A Life in Pictures, Jack
Nicholson recalled a time when Kubrick forced

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:31.400
Duvall to film a particularly grueling scene
127 times.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.440
"I'm very confused.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.940
I just want a chance to think things over."

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:43.530
All those takes took their toll: Duvall was
dehydrated from crying so much, and clumps

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.169
of hair fell out of her head.There are even
hints that she considered quitting due to

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:48.200
health concerns.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.479
In the book The Complete Kubrick, Duvall states,
"I was really in and out of ill health because

00:06:52.479 --> 00:06:54.440
the stress of the role was so great…

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:56.860
It's the most difficult role I've ever had
to play."

00:06:57.460 --> 00:07:02.000
Even before legendary comedian Lily Tomlin
signed on the play Violet Newstead in 9 to

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.319
5, she had her doubts.

00:07:03.319 --> 00:07:06.949
Jane Fonda, who co-produced the cult classic,
spent the better part of a year convincing

00:07:06.949 --> 00:07:08.430
Tomlin to come on board.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.740
Tomlin told People, "I didn't want to do a
cheap comedy.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.639
I was looking for something more serious."

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:16.560
After she signed on, Tomlin caught a glimpse
of her performance in a fantasy scene where

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.920
she was acting with as-yet nonexistent cartoon
birds and thought she was horrible in it.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:24.420
"Thank you."

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.020
She promptly called the producer, offered
to give her salary back, and begged to be

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:29.520
let go.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:33.300
Luckily, Tomlin caught more footage from later
scenes and decided to stay on.

